sec.p:
In this study, to extract sequence information from multiple perspectives, we employ several different kinds of encoding methods (as shown in Table 2). First, we leverage the ESM-2 model to obtain pre-trained feature representations of peptide sequences42. In this process, each amino acid is represented as a vector with 1280 dimensions, resulting in a feature matrix M with dimensions of L×1280, where L denotes the peptide sequence length. The ESM-2 language model, pre-trained on a large-scale protein sequence dataset, is capable of capturing rich contextual information and complex sequence patterns, providing powerful feature representations for downstream tasks. In addition, we incorporate three other types of amino acid information to represent the peptide sequences: one-hot encoding, BLOSUM62 encoding43, and Z-scale encoding44. One-hot encoding converts each amino acid residue categorical value into a vector with length equal to the total number of categories, where only the element corresponding to the specific category is 1, and all other elements are 0. BLOSUM62 encoding transforms each peptide sequence into an L×20 numerical matrix. This numerical matrix not only captures the rich evolutionary information of amino acid residues but also reflects the trends of residue substitution in the process of natural selection. Z-scale encoding transforms each peptide sequence into an L×5 numerical matrix. Z-scale encoding is based on the Z-scale normalization method, which standardizes and compares various physicochemical properties of amino acids. As a result, Z-scale encoding captures the physical and chemical properties of the amino acids in the peptide sequence. By integrating the one-hot, BLOSUM62, and Z-scale features, this study constructs a feature matrix \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
