				\begin{document}$$\:{H}^{\left(0\right)}$$\end{document} with dimensions of L×45.

We employ a contrastive learning approach to train a Multi-Layer Perceptron (MLP) encoder, thereby enhancing the discriminative ability of pre-trained features. Contrastive Learning45 is a feature learning method based on representation space optimization, which enhances the model’s representational ability by constructing a discriminative feature space. The core of this method lies in learning a metric space such that embeddings of similar samples are positioned close together, while embeddings of dissimilar samples are kept far apart. This dual optimization objective is achieved by concurrently maximizing the distance between different classes and minimizing the distance within the same class, significantly improving the discriminative power of peptide embeddings. In our framework, the contrastive pairs are constructed in a supervised manner rather than by data augmentation. Specifically, two sequences belonging to the same class (both AMPs or both non-AMPs) are regarded as a positive pair, whereas sequences from different classes (one AMP and one non-AMP) form a negative pair. This design enables the model to explicitly learn class-level discriminative representations instead of relying on stochastic sequence perturbations. In this study, we adopt binary labels \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
