The Gated Convolutional Neural Network (GCNN) was originally proposed for language modeling tasks38. It integrates Convolutional Neural Networks (CNN) with Gated Linear Units (GLU), a type of gating mechanism, ultimately constructing an efficient and powerful framework for sequence modeling. Unlike Recurrent Neural Networks (RNN), which rely on sequential computations based on time steps, the GCNN extracts local features through parallelized convolution operations and introduces gating mechanisms at the channel level to enhance feature selection and information flow control. Notably, by coupling the gating mechanism with linear transformations, GCNN enables the network to retain hierarchical non-linear modeling capabilities while providing a linear path for gradient transmission. This effectively alleviates the vanishing gradient problem in deep networks. In tasks involving long-term dependencies, the GCNN demonstrates higher computational efficiency compared to traditional methods such as Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM), owing to its parallel computing capabilities and hierarchical structure. Specifically, by introducing the gating mechanism, GCNN further optimizes the control of information flow. This not only enhances the model’s ability to capture long-range dependencies but also significantly improves performance in complex sequence modeling tasks. We extract the contextual information of peptide sequences using GCNN, where the sequence embeddings are represented by multi-dimensional features. In this study, we designed a three-layer GCNN module to extract contextual information from peptide sequences. Each layer consists of three parallel 1D convolutional branches with kernel sizes of 3, 5, and 7, enabling multi-scale receptive field learning. The input to each convolutional layer has 256 channels, and each convolution produces 512 channels, which are processed through the GLU mechanism to enhance feature selection and control information flow. No dilation is applied. Residual connections are added after each GCNN block to alleviate the vanishing gradient problem and enhance training stability. Finally, the outputs of the three convolutional branches are concatenated to form the overall representation of the module. The formula is as follows:4\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
